const miftahPageTranslations = [
`Adam used to wake up every day before sunrise—not because he had a job, but because he was looking for one. He was twenty-seven, a civil engineer who had graduated three years earlier. His degree hung on the wall above his bed, and every morning he would look at it and wonder whether all his hard work had only led to sitting at home.

His family lived in a small house in a Gaza neighborhood. His father had once worked in construction, but age and painful knees kept him off the scaffolding. His mother sewed for women in the neighborhood to help with the household expenses.

That morning, his mother asked about another interview. A company near the port needed a site engineer, Adam explained, but wanted five years of experience. “How are you supposed to get experience when they all want experience?” she asked. Adam laughed lightly, though his heart was not laughing.

He put his certificates in a blue folder and left. Before closing the door, he imagined returning one day with good news—his mother ululating and his father lifting his head with pride. His dream was bigger than a salary: he wanted the key to a home, a wife waiting for him, and children calling him “Baba.”`,

`Adam reached the company after taking two shared taxis and walking for half an hour. Ten other young men sat in the old office, each carrying a folder much like his. He sat beside Samer, another civil engineer who had spent two years searching for work. Samer had once worked unpaid for two months as a “trainee,” only to be replaced by a relative of the company owner.

When Adam’s turn came, three men questioned him about his studies, engineering software, and ability to work under pressure. He answered confidently, but they told him his theoretical knowledge was good and his experience insufficient. He offered to prove himself for a month without pay. Their cold answer was, “We’ll call you.” Adam knew that usually meant, “Don’t wait.”

He walked to the sea and sat near the water. “I’m not asking for a miracle,” he said. “I just want to begin.” When his mother called, he could not tell her the truth. He said the interview had gone well and repeated the familiar words: “They said they’ll call.” She answered hopefully, “God willing, it will be good.” She did not know he had heard that same sentence more than fifty times.`,

`On his way home, Adam passed the small bookshop where he used to buy notebooks at university. Nour, his former classmate, stood behind the counter. She had studied interior design but now worked with her father in the shop. She welcomed him warmly and immediately understood the blue folder under his arm: another interview, and probably the same old answer.

She made him coffee because he looked as if he were carrying the worries of the whole world. Adam had loved her for years but had never told her. How could he talk about marriage with no steady job, no home, and not even enough money for a ring?

Nour told him not to let rejection convince him that he was worthless. Perhaps, she suggested, the path he was seeking was not the one he needed. Why wait for a company to give him a chance? Why not begin with something small on his own? Adam joked bitterly that he had only seven shekels in his pocket. Nour replied that not every beginning needs an office and a sign; sometimes it only needs someone who believes in himself. Her words stayed with him all the way home.`,

`The next day, Adam was sitting at the front door sketching a plan in an old notebook when their neighbor Abu Mohammad arrived. He wanted to enlarge a room on his roof and asked Adam to check whether the column could support it. Adam measured the spaces, examined the cracks, and studied the old plans. The extension was possible, he concluded, but part of the ceiling needed reinforcement.

Abu Mohammad asked Adam to take charge of the work. Adam thought he was joking, but the neighbor trusted him as both an engineer and a son of the neighborhood. Adam went to his father, frightened because this would be his first job alone. His father told him that fear did not mean he was incapable; it meant he understood the weight of responsibility. He should study the job carefully and never pretend to know what he did not.

Within a week, Adam prepared the calculations and plans, and his father helped him choose workers and materials. Adam worked from dawn until sunset. When the first wall rose, he ran his hand over the cement and felt that he was building more than a room. He was building the first step in the staircase of his life.`,

`Abu Mohammad’s project succeeded, and the neighborhood began talking about the young engineer who stood beside the workers and checked every detail. A week later, a widow named Umm Khaled asked him to repair her leaking kitchen roof. Knowing she had little money, Adam charged her only for materials. When she said his labor had value, he replied that her blessing was worth more.

His father warned him that generosity was good, but he also had to live. Adam needed to learn the difference between kindness and giving away what was rightfully his.

Adam created an online page for his engineering supervision and renovation work. Nour liked the name but said the logo needed work, so he asked her to design one. She made a simple white house whose roof formed the Arabic letter alif, with Adam’s name underneath. Looking at it, Adam felt his name was no longer written only on a degree hanging on a wall. Now it was attached to real work.`,

`Two months later, Adam received his largest project yet: renovating an old house for a large family. He agreed on the price and schedule, then paid for materials from his own pocket. Prices rose, however, and the owner delayed payment. Workers demanded their wages, the supplier wanted payment for the steel and cement, and every night Adam’s calculations showed the same result: he was losing money.

The owner refused to pay anything more until the job was finished, even though they had agreed on three installments. “Deal with it. You’re the contractor,” he told Adam. For the first time, Adam felt his dream might collapse. He went home, threw the folder on the table, and announced that he had failed.

After hearing every detail, his father said Adam had been wrong to start without a clear contract and to spend too much of his own money. But making a mistake did not make him a failure. A failure repeats the same mistake. Adam had paid the price of a lesson; now he had to decide whether to learn from it or waste that price.`,

`Adam called Nour and asked her to meet him by the sea. Sitting on the seawall in the cold wind, he told her about the project, the debts, and his fear of returning to zero. He had thought he was approaching his dream, but now felt farther away.

When Nour asked what his dream really was, Adam finally answered: steady work, a home, someone waiting at the end of the day, a family and children, and a life not ruled by fear of tomorrow. Then he admitted that whenever he imagined that home, he saw Nour in it.

Nour asked why he had never spoken. Adam said he had nothing—no guaranteed job, apartment, or money. She told him a family did not have to be built after everything else was finished; perhaps family was what helped people build everything else. She did not want a palace, only an honest man who worked hard and rose whenever he fell. For the first time, Adam felt his dream was not standing far away. It was sitting beside him.`,

`The next day, Adam went calmly to the homeowner with a full list of materials, photographs of the work, and their old agreement. He admitted his mistake in not writing a detailed contract and confronted the owner’s mistake in stopping payment. If the second installment was paid, he promised to finish within ten days.

After a long discussion, the owner paid part of the amount and signed a new contract. Adam paid the workers, resumed the job, recorded every expense, and delivered the house eleven days later. He made little profit after paying his debts, but most importantly, he did not lose his good name.

Adam learned never to start without a contract, buy materials without an advance, or feel embarrassed to ask for what he was owed. He partnered with Samer: Adam managed the sites while Samer handled drawings and calculations. Nour designed their new logo with the line, “We build on the right foundation.”`,

`A year passed. Adam was not rich and had not opened a large company, but he had steady, reasonable work. He saved part of every payment in a small box he called “the house fund.” One evening he told his parents he wanted to ask for Nour’s hand. His delighted mother said she had known for a long time. His father asked whether he could establish a home. Adam did not have everything, he replied, but he had work, a plan, and the willingness to struggle.

At Nour’s family home, her father asked about Adam’s plan. Adam opened a blue folder—the same color as the one he once carried from company to company. This time it held no certificates. Inside were work records, expenses, a two-year plan, and a drawing of a small apartment above his parents’ home.

“I can’t promise you a life without hardship,” Adam said, “but I promise I will not run away from it.” One week later, the two families recited Al-Fatiha to mark the engagement.`,

`Adam began building the apartment above his parents’ home, acting as engineer, contractor, and sometimes laborer. His father watched from the roof and jokingly called Adam an “engineer of paper” while calling himself an engineer of sun and dust. Nour chose colors, designed the kitchen, and insisted that the window be placed where morning light could enter. It was her home too.

Six months later, the small apartment was finished. On their wedding night, Adam placed the key in Nour’s hand. “This isn’t the key to my home,” he told her. “It’s the key to our home.” Inside, they hung his old degree, Nour’s logo, and placed the blue folder beneath them. It contained every rejection letter, job application, first contract, and first drawing of the apartment—a reminder that the doors closed in Adam’s face had taught him how to build a door of his own.

Two years later, Adam and Samer opened a modest office. When young engineers came looking for work, Adam remembered waiting in corridors and hearing, “We’ll call you.” He offered paid training whenever he could. They could not solve unemployment, he said, but they did not have to become copies of those who had shut the door on them.

One Friday evening, Adam returned home to his little son running toward him shouting, “Baba! Baba is home!” With the child in his arms and Nour smiling from the kitchen, Adam looked at the window, his degree, the logo, and the blue folder. He understood that work had never been the whole dream. Work was the road. The dream was this home—and the voice that called him “Baba.”`
];

