  `One week after her grandmother Um Salim died, the whole family gathered at the old house to sort through her belongings. The house was spacious, but the silence inside it felt even greater. Every corner carried her grandmother’s scent: coffee, soap, and the old perfume that lingered on her abaya.

Reem, her eldest granddaughter, stood before a dark wooden wardrobe in the bedroom. She reached for the handle, but her mother, Najwa, shouted from behind her and told her to leave it alone. Her voice was harsher than necessary. Reem said nothing, but kept staring at the handle.

Ever since Reem was a child, her grandmother had forbidden anyone from opening that wardrobe, claiming it contained old things of no value. But once, when Reem was ten, she had seen her grandmother standing in front of it, holding a photograph and crying.

That night, after everyone had gone home, Reem stayed at the house with her mother so they could sleep over at her uncle Salim’s. But she could not fall asleep. At around two in the morning, she quietly got up, entered her grandmother’s room, and opened the wardrobe.

Behind a pile of abayas, she found a small metal box. Inside were letters tied with a green ribbon, a silver necklace, and an old family photograph. The picture showed her grandmother, her grandfather, her mother Najwa as a little girl, and her uncle Salim. But there was an empty space between her grandparents—the place where someone had been cut out of the photograph with scissors.`,
  `Reem sat on the edge of the bed, her heart pounding. She picked up the first letter; its paper was yellow and its ink had faded. Written on the front were the words: “To my mother… from your daughter Samar.” Reem read the sentence twice. Samar? There was no one named Samar in the family. As far as everyone knew, her grandmother had only one daughter, Najwa, and one son, Salim.

Reem opened the letter. Samar wrote that she knew her mother was angry with her, but swore that what happened had been beyond her control. She did not know where to go or who would believe her, and she wanted Najwa to know that she had never hated her. Reem paused at her mother’s name, then continued reading.

Samar wrote that the family had been told she ran away, but that was not true. She left because there was no longer any place for her in the house. She warned her mother not to believe Abu Fayez if anything happened to her.

Before Reem could continue, the bedroom light came on. Najwa stood in the doorway, her face drained of color. In a choked voice she demanded to know where Reem had found the letter, then snatched it from her hand. When Reem asked who Samar was and whether she was her aunt, Najwa told her it was none of her business.

Reem raised her voice, insisting that this was her family too. Najwa gathered the letters with trembling hands and said the story had died long ago. Reem asked why her grandmother had kept the letters hidden if that were true. After a silence, Najwa replied, “Because some things do not die. We are simply forced to bury them.”`,
  `The next day, Reem confronted her uncle Salim. He was sitting in the old house’s living room, counting the money found in their grandmother’s box. She placed the photograph before him and asked who had been cut out of it. His hand froze. When she pressed him about Samar, he asked whether her mother had told her.

After a long silence, Salim admitted that Samar was their older sister. Reem felt as though the house had tilted beneath her. He said no one knew where Samar was: thirty years earlier, after a major family conflict, she had left the house and never returned. When Reem asked if she had run away, Salim answered only that this was what people had been told.

Reem revealed that Samar’s letters said she had not run away. Alarmed that Reem had read them, Salim began pacing and warned her to leave the matter alone. Some things, he said, cannot be closed again once they are opened.

When Reem mentioned Abu Fayez, Salim froze. In a low voice, he explained that Abu Fayez had been their grandfather’s friend and a frequent visitor to the house. After Samar disappeared, he left the country. Salim claimed not to know why.

Reem accused him of lying. Salim angrily warned her to watch how she spoke to him. She answered that the family should watch how they buried a person who might still be alive.`,
  `Reem found an old address in one of the letters. It led to the home of a woman named Um Fayez in Gaza’s Shuja’iyya neighborhood. Reem went there with her younger brother Ziad, who was five years younger and had been excited about the mystery from the moment he heard it.

As they walked, Ziad imagined discovering an aunt in another country, perhaps with children of her own—relatives they had never known existed. Reem bluntly reminded him that Samar might be dead. They found an old house with a green door, where an elderly woman answered their knock.

The woman explained that Um Fayez had died five years earlier and that she was her daughter-in-law. When Reem showed her the old photograph, the woman immediately recognized Samar. She said Samar had visited Um Fayez often, but then all news of her had suddenly stopped.

Asked about Abu Fayez, the woman described him as a dangerous man who worked with people in trade and seemed to have far more money than he should. He had moved to Jordan after the incident involving Samar.

When Reem asked what had happened, the woman lowered her eyes and revealed what people had heard: Samar had been pregnant. Ziad gasped. Reem asked who the father was, but the woman returned the photograph and said that very question was what had torn the whole family apart.`,
  `Reem returned home and confronted her mother. Najwa was peeling potatoes in the kitchen, but the knife fell from her hand when she heard what Reem had learned. Reem asked directly whether Samar had been pregnant and demanded the truth.

Najwa said she had been seventeen at the time, while Samar was seven years older. Samar had been the most beautiful girl in the neighborhood and the brightest of them all. She was not engaged, but a young man named Marwan loved her. His family rejected her. Later, Samar told her mother that she was pregnant and said Marwan was the father.

Their father did not believe her because Abu Fayez claimed Marwan had left the country before the pregnancy could have begun. Najwa admitted that she had believed no one; she had been young and afraid. Their father beat Samar and called her a disgrace to the family. Their mother tried to defend her but could not, and Samar disappeared the following day.

Najwa insisted that they had searched for Samar and that her mother had kept looking for years. But when Reem asked what Najwa herself had done, her silence gave away the answer. Through tears, Najwa admitted she had not searched because she had been jealous of her sister.

Everyone had loved Samar. Their father was proud of her, their mother shared her secrets with her, and Najwa had always felt like the second daughter. When the scandal happened, part of her felt that Samar had finally fallen from the place she occupied above her. Najwa covered her face and confessed that this feeling had tormented her for thirty years.`,
  `Reem searched through the letters again and found one that had never been opened. It bore an Amman postmark and was dated seven years after Samar’s disappearance. Reem opened it carefully.

Samar wrote that she was alive and had a daughter named Maha. She had tried many times to return, but Abu Fayez threatened to hurt Maha if she did. Maha was innocent. Samar was living in Amman and working in a sewing workshop, and she longed to see her mother before she died.

Reem read Maha’s name again and again. Samar had a daughter. That meant Najwa had a niece, and Reem had a cousin. At the end of the letter was an old address and a telephone number with two digits missing.

When Najwa read the letter, her legs gave way and she sank to the floor. Her mother had known Samar was alive. Reem suggested the letter might have arrived after her grandfather became ill and that her grandmother had been afraid, but Najwa pointed out that her father was already dead by then. Her mother could have spoken. Perhaps, Najwa said, she had been waiting for the right time. Reem replied that the right time had passed thirty years ago.

That night, Reem and Ziad agreed to search for Maha. They used her name and the old address, contacted people living in Amman, and posted a message in an online family group. Four days later, a woman named Rasha replied. She knew Maha, but before revealing where she was, she wanted to know whether they belonged to her mother’s family.`,
  `Reem had a long phone conversation with Rasha and learned that Maha was a thirty-nine-year-old teacher living in Zarqa. Samar, however, had died six years earlier after a long illness. When Najwa heard the news, she said nothing, went into her room, and closed the door.

The next day, Maha called. Her voice was calm but cold. After confirming that she was speaking to Reem, Najwa’s daughter, Maha said her mother had often spoken about Najwa. Samar had said that she loved her, but also that Najwa had stood silently by when the family cast her out.

Reem told Maha that her mother was remorseful. Maha replied that remorse could not bring back the dead. She demanded to know why the family had searched for her only now: because their grandmother had died, because the letters had surfaced, or simply to ease their own consciences.

Reem answered honestly that she had carried neither a clear nor a guilty conscience because she had never known Maha existed. Now that she did, she wanted to know her. Maha admitted she was not sure she wanted to know any of them.

Before ending the call, Reem asked who Maha’s father was. After a long silence, Maha said her mother had told her it was Marwan. She had believed Samar because her mother had never lied to her. Yet before dying, Samar had admitted that part of the story was missing—something she had never managed to reveal.`,
  `Through Rasha, Reem learned that Abu Fayez was still alive in a nursing home in Amman. Najwa refused to go because she could not bear to see his face. Reem argued that he might be the only person who knew the truth. When Salim wondered whether the truth might be worse than what they already knew, Reem replied that it was worse when left incomplete.

A week later, Reem traveled with Salim to see Abu Fayez. They found a frail man whose voice was barely audible. His expression changed when he saw Samar’s photograph, and he recognized her as Mahmoud’s daughter.

Abu Fayez asked why Reem wanted to open graves when everyone involved was dead. She answered that they had buried living people alongside the dead. Pressed by Salim, Abu Fayez finally admitted that Samar had been pregnant by Marwan.

He explained that Marwan was his business partner’s son. Marwan’s family wanted him to marry a merchant’s daughter, and news of the pregnancy would have ruined the arrangement. Abu Fayez had therefore lied to Reem’s grandfather and claimed Marwan had already left the country. When Reem accused him of destroying a young woman’s life for a deal, he said nothing.

Abu Fayez also admitted why he had threatened Samar after she left. Marwan died in an accident a year later, and his family did not want anyone claiming a share of the inheritance. Reem thought the whole story was finally clear, but Abu Fayez raised his head and said there was still something Samar herself had never known.`,
  `Before Abu Fayez could continue, a nurse entered and said he was exhausted and needed to rest. Reem tried to question him, but he closed his eyes and stopped responding. When they returned to the nursing home the next day, they learned that he had died during the night.

The nurse handed them an envelope that Abu Fayez had asked her to give them if anything happened to him. Inside, Reem found an old document recording Marwan’s parentage, a photograph of him, and a short letter.

Abu Fayez revealed that Marwan was not his business partner’s biological son. He was the son of the partner’s brother, who had died young, and had merely been raised in the partner’s home. Marwan therefore had no right to the inheritance his family had feared losing. Everything that had happened had been built on a lie.

Reem read the words repeatedly. Samar had been cast out, Marwan had died, Maha had grown up without a father, and the family had been torn apart—all over an inheritance claim that had never existed. Back home, Najwa said Samar had died believing Marwan had abandoned her. Reem corrected her: Maha said Samar knew he had died. What Samar never knew was that the entire story surrounding them had been a lie.

For the first time in front of them, Salim wept. He admitted that he had watched his father beat Samar and had stood silently by. He had been young then, but had remained silent even after growing up. Najwa approached her brother, and for the first time they embraced—not because they had forgiven each other, but because the pain was too great for either of them to carry alone.`, '', ''
