  `Almost a month had passed since Adam’s first day at work, and the office no longer felt as unfamiliar. He knew where to put his bag, which chair squeaked, when Muhammad went out for coffee, and when Suha began losing patience with the constantly ringing phone.

During his first week, Adam had been afraid to press any button in the software without asking. Now he entered orders, printed invoices, and answered simple calls by himself. He still made mistakes, but no longer thought each one would cost him his job. He understood that work was not a single exam: every day brought something new, and every mistake could become a lesson if he paid attention.

He came home tired but happy. His days now had shape and meaning. What he thought about most was his salary—not because it was large, but because it would be the first money he had earned through his own effort. He imagined giving part of it to his mother and buying something useful for his father.

His sister Nour kept reminding him that she expected a gift. She joked that she had been the first to congratulate him, while Adam reminded her that she had actually been the first to make fun of his formal clothes. Even so, he genuinely wanted to buy her something.

A week before payday, Adam wrote a list: money for the household, new shoes for his father, perfume for his mother, headphones for Nour, clothes for himself, and some savings. After estimating every price, he discovered that his plans cost more than the salary itself. Laughing to himself, Adam learned his first lesson before even receiving his first paycheck: money disappears much faster than people imagine.`,
  `The next day, Adam took his notebook to work and reviewed his budget during lunch. Muhammad read the list and laughed, saying Adam would spend his salary before receiving it. Adam defended his plan: he had not bought anything for himself in a long time and wanted to make his family happy.

Muhammad agreed but advised him to save part of the salary first, even a small amount. Asked about his own first paycheck, Muhammad admitted buying an expensive phone and then borrowing money for transportation for the rest of the month. He regretted it whenever he held the new phone but could not afford a sandwich.

Suha joined the conversation. Her first salary had all gone to the household because her father was ill. Later, her mother secretly used part of that money to buy Suha a bag. Suha kept it for over five years, not because it was expensive but because it came from her first salary.

Adam wanted to do everything: buy gifts, purchase something for himself, help at home, and save. But there was only one salary. He crossed new clothes off the list, replaced the expensive headphones with a simple gift for Nour, and wrote a new rule: do not spend everything on the same day.

The plan now felt better, but Adam did not yet know that the amount he would receive was smaller than the amount he had calculated.`,
  `Before working, Adam imagined his entire salary as money he could keep. After one month, he understood that work itself had costs: two shared-taxi fares each day and, when he could not prepare food, a sandwich or coffee. At first he ignored these small expenses, but by month’s end his old savings were nearly gone.

One Wednesday, he saw a white shirt he liked in a shop window. He considered trying it on, but remembered that payday had not arrived and his remaining money had to last through the week. For the first time, he walked away from something he wanted because he had begun thinking about priorities.

At home, he found his mother washing dishes beside an envelope and two bills. She counted her money twice, clearly unsure how to pay the bills and buy household supplies. His father came in, and she quietly explained that the electricity bill was higher than last month.

Nobody asked Adam for money. His father had told him that his salary belonged to him and that he should learn to manage it. His mother had never mentioned money at all. Still, seeing the bills changed his plan.

In his room, Adam opened his notebook, crossed out the amount beside “money for the household,” and replaced it with a larger one.`,
  `Thursday, the last workday of the month, finally arrived. Adam’s mother recognized payday from his smile, while Nour admitted she had been counting down for four days and reminded him about her gift. Adam joked that continued reminders would earn her only a bag of chips.

On the way to work, Adam imagined receiving a white envelope filled with crisp banknotes. At the office, everyone discussed payday. Muhammad joked that his entire salary would go straight to the grocer who had given him credit all month. Suha planned to buy her niece a sweater, while Rami tried to finish the payroll calculations.

Half the day passed without payment. Adam tried to work but looked up whenever Mr. Nader’s door opened. At three, Rami began calling employees one by one. Muhammad emerged first with an envelope, followed by Suha and the others.

Finally Rami called Adam. Mr. Nader sat behind his desk with an envelope bearing Adam’s name. He pushed it toward him, smiled, and wished that his first salary would mark a fortunate beginning.

The envelope felt lighter than Adam had imagined. Yet at that moment, it felt as though he held an entire month of effort between his hands.`,
  `After leaving Mr. Nader’s office, Adam put the envelope in his bag and tried to keep working. At the end of the shift, he opened it in the break room and counted the money twice. The amount was lower than the salary discussed during his interview. Confused, he counted a third time and got the same result.

Adam worried that the company had deducted money because of his first-day mistake. He wanted to ask Rami but felt embarrassed, afraid of appearing obsessed with every penny immediately after being paid. Muhammad noticed his expression and asked what was wrong.

When Adam quietly explained, Muhammad asked when he had started. Adam had begun four days after the start of the month, so his first salary covered only the days he had actually worked. Adam checked the date and felt relieved.

Still, the amount was smaller than his plan. On the way home, he reviewed the list again. He refused to reduce the household contribution, but the gifts could be simpler and new clothes could wait another month. He wrote: “I don’t have to do everything with my first salary.”

Although the amount was lower than expected, it still meant a great deal. This money had not been given to him as a gift; he had worked for it.`,
  `Instead of going straight home, Adam stopped at the market to buy gifts. Choosing them proved harder than expected. He first looked for work shoes for his father, but good pairs exceeded his budget and the cheaper pair looked poorly made, so he left without buying.

At a perfume shop, he remembered that his mother disliked strong scents. He found an affordable set containing a light perfume and hand cream and had it gift-wrapped. For Nour, he chose a pretty notebook and a funny mug. Good headphones were too expensive, but he reminded himself that a gift’s value was not its price.

He still needed something for his father. Passing a shop selling watches, wallets, and belts, he noticed a simple leather wallet and remembered that his father’s old one was torn. The price was suitable, so he bought it.

At the edge of the market, Adam counted what remained. The household contribution was safe, but almost nothing was left for himself. He looked once more toward the shop displaying the white shirt he liked.

Adam stood at the window, smiled, and walked away, telling himself, “Next month.”`,
  `At the building entrance, Adam divided his salary: the household contribution stayed in the envelope, some went into his wallet for transport and weekly expenses, and a smaller amount went into an inner pocket as savings. The full envelope had emptied quickly.

At home, Nour immediately asked about her gift. Adam pretended he had forgotten, then surprised her with the notebook and mug. She noted that they were not the headphones she requested, but quickly hugged the notebook when he offered to return them.

Adam gave his mother the perfume set. She initially refused, saying he should save his money, but accepted it after he insisted. She smiled at the scent and prayed for blessing in his livelihood.

Then Adam placed the salary envelope on the table and said, “This is for the household.”

His mother’s smile faded slightly. She did not reach for it.`,
  `The envelope remained between them. His mother insisted that the salary belonged to Adam after a year of waiting for work. Adam explained that he had kept some and saved some, but she still refused.

Adam told her he had seen the electricity bills and understood the household expenses. Nobody had asked him to pay, but his family had supported him throughout unemployment. He could not repay everything; he simply wanted to contribute.

Tearful, his mother finally accepted the unopened envelope and prayed for blessing in his livelihood.

When his father returned, Adam gave him the new wallet. His father quietly opened it and ran a finger over the leather before saying, “A first salary is never forgotten.”

Adam understood that his father was not talking about money, but about the entire moment.`,
  `On Friday morning, Adam found Nour using her new mug and his father already using the wallet. After breakfast, he discovered the household envelope inside his work bag. It contained part of the money and a note from his mother: “Save this. What you gave us is enough for the household.”

His mother explained that she had kept enough to help with the bills but would not accept his entire salary. Learning how to save was as important as learning how to spend, because he could not know what he might need in a month or a year.

Adam added the returned money to his small savings. He opened his notebook and titled a new page “Saved money.”

The amount was small, but it was the first money he had saved from his own work. Beneath it, he wrote his goal: buy a laptop for work and learning.

Giving the savings a goal made the small amount feel more meaningful.`,
  `That night, Adam sat on the roof with his notebook. His original salary plan was covered in crossed-out items and changed numbers. Before payday, he had imagined his first salary would solve everything. Now he understood that money was not magic; a person had to choose what mattered, what could wait, what to save, and how to help.

He remembered his mother smiling at the perfume, his father using the wallet, and Nour drinking from the mug while still talking about headphones. He also remembered the household envelope and the money his mother returned.

The salary was not large and could not cover everything he wanted. Yet it made him feel able to help, responsible, and certain that his effort had value even when the amount was modest.

Looking at the darkening homes, Adam told himself that next month he wanted to be better—not only at work, but at working hard, spending, saving, and appreciating what he had.

His first salary remained in his memory not because of its amount, but because it was the first time he felt he had begun taking part of his life into his own hands.`
