jarJdidPageTranslations[2] = `The next day was Saturday. Adam woke up late with a headache from lack of sleep. When he entered the kitchen, he found Nour sitting at the table, clearly in no mood to deal with anyone. Their mother was preparing breakfast and tried to convince them that the neighbors had needed to arrange their belongings. Nour was not convinced. She said people could do that in the morning, not at two o’clock at night. Adam partly agreed, but he did not want to start a problem with new neighbors on their very first day.

After breakfast, Adam went up to the roof. Sameh was there, sitting with a cup of coffee. The moment he saw Adam, he laughed and asked, “Did you sleep well?” Adam understood that Sameh had heard the noise too. He sat beside him and said that he had barely slept. Sameh said the noise had reached their apartment as well, even though they were on the second floor. Then he laughed and said, “If that was only the first night, God help us with what’s coming.” Adam laughed, but he did not like the thought.

Saturday passed quietly, and Adam thought the matter was over. But on Sunday, he returned from work tired. As soon as he entered his room to rest, loud music began playing upstairs. It was quiet at first, then grew louder after five minutes. Adam tried to ignore it. He opened his laptop and began reviewing some work, but every time he tried to concentrate, the music filled his head.

The noise stopped around sunset, and Adam sighed with relief. But after dinner, it started again. This time, he could hear young men laughing along with the music. Nour came out of her room and told Adam to speak to them. He said it was still early and not yet late. But at a quarter to eleven, the music was still playing. His father sat silently in the living room, glancing at the ceiling every few minutes.

Eventually, the music stopped and the home became quiet again. But something Nour said stayed in Adam’s mind: “They live above us. If we don’t say anything now, they’ll think this is fine.” Adam did not answer, because he had begun thinking the same thing.`;
