jarJdidPageTranslations[3] = `The next morning, Adam left home early for work. As he closed the door, he heard someone coming down the stairs. It was Yousef, dressed for work and holding car keys. They greeted each other and walked downstairs together.

Adam considered mentioning the noise. He opened his mouth to begin, but before he could speak, Yousef asked where he worked. They began talking about their jobs, and Adam learned that Yousef worked at an electrical-appliance store. Yousef had worked there for two years, and because the store was far from the new home, he had to leave early every day.

At the building entrance, Yousef asked whether Adam knew the quickest way to the transport stop. Adam explained the route and told him about another, closer stop. Yousef thanked him. Before they parted, Adam again considered mentioning the music, but said nothing. He did not know why. Perhaps because Yousef was friendly, or because Adam did not want their first conversation to make him look like a neighbor who complained.

Adam thought about it throughout his journey to work. The solution was simple: go to Yousef and calmly explain that the noise carried into their apartment. That was all. But sometimes the simplest conversation was harder than the problem itself.

At work, Muhammad noticed that Adam was distracted and asked what was wrong. Adam told him about the new neighbor and the music. Muhammad laughed. “Come on, just talk to him. You’re not going there to fight.” Adam explained that they were new and he did not want to begin the relationship with a complaint.

Muhammad thought for a moment. “If you speak politely, it isn’t a complaint. You’re only letting him know that the sound reaches your apartment.” That made sense. Adam decided that if it happened again, he would speak to him. He did not know that what happened that night would make the conversation harder than he expected.`;
