jarJdidPageTranslations[4] = `At around nine that night, Adam was having dinner with his family. The home was quiet. His father was talking about work, Nour was holding her phone beneath the table, and her mother kept asking her to put it away.

Suddenly, they heard a loud thud. Everyone fell silent. Then they heard something rolling across the floor above them. Nour looked at the ceiling. A minute later, the banging returned: thud… thud… thud.

Abu Adam set his spoon on the table, visibly upset this time. Adam said he would go upstairs to see what was happening. He stopped outside the neighbors’ door. Before knocking, he heard sounds from inside. It was not music. A man was moving around quickly, and a frightened woman was speaking.

Adam knocked, but nobody answered. He knocked again. A few seconds later, Yousef opened the door. His face was tense and his hair disheveled. Adam had been ready to mention the noise, but the moment he saw Yousef’s face, he fell silent. “What happened?” he asked.

Yousef looked behind him, then said that his father had fallen. He quickly explained that his father had been trying to take a box down from the top of the wardrobe and had slipped off a chair.

Without thinking, Adam went inside. Abu Yousef was sitting on the floor beside the wardrobe, holding his leg, while Umm Yousef stood beside him in confusion. Adam asked whether he could stand. Abu Yousef tried, but the moment he placed weight on his leg, his face changed with pain.

Adam hurried downstairs and called his father. Minutes later, Abu Adam, Abu Sameh, and Sameh were all in the apartment. They helped Abu Yousef down to the car, and Yousef drove him to the hospital.

Adam remained at the building entrance, watching until the car disappeared at the end of the street. He remembered why he had gone upstairs: to complain about the banging. But now the very same noise no longer annoyed him. It had taken on another meaning.`;
