jarJdidPageTranslations[5] = `The next day, they learned that Abu Yousef’s leg was not broken, but badly sprained, and the doctor had told him to rest for several weeks.

Umm Adam made soup, put it in a small pot, and asked Adam to take it upstairs to their neighbors. This time, he did not object. He knocked and Yousef opened the door. His face looked tired, but calmer than the previous night.

Adam went inside. Abu Yousef sat on the sofa with his leg raised on a pillow. He thanked Adam for helping and said that Adam’s father, Sameh, and Abu Sameh had stood by them as though they had known them for years. Adam said that was only natural—they were neighbors.

Adam stayed for a while. During the conversation, he learned more about the family. Abu Yousef worked as a carpenter, but work had been scarce for months. That was why they had left their old home for an apartment with lower rent. Yousef was carrying a large share of the household expenses. He also had a younger brother named Hamza who was still at school and spent most of his time in his room, which was why Adam had hardly seen him.

Near the end of their conversation, Yousef laughed and said, “By the way, I know we disturbed you during our first few days.” Adam was surprised. Yousef continued, explaining that his mother had warned him about the music, but he had thought the sound would not reach the apartment below.

Adam calmly said that it did reach them, especially at night. Yousef apologized immediately. He did not defend himself or accuse Adam of exaggerating. He simply said, “You’re right, and I’m sorry. It won’t happen again.”

Adam felt relieved. All the anxiety he had felt about this conversation turned out to be unnecessary. Before he left, Yousef thanked him again for helping his father.

As Adam walked downstairs, he thought about how easily someone could form a complete picture of another person without knowing anything about them. Two days earlier, Yousef had been only “the noisy neighbor” in Adam’s mind. Now he had a name, a job, a family, and worries like anyone else.`;
jarJdidClauseTranslations[5] = ['The next day,','they learned that Abu Yousef’s leg was not broken,','but badly sprained,','and the doctor had told him to rest for several weeks.','Umm Adam made soup,','put it in a small pot,','and asked Adam to take it upstairs to their neighbors.','This time,','he did not object.','He knocked on the door,','and Yousef opened it.','His face looked tired,','but calmer than the previous night.','Adam went inside.','Abu Yousef was sitting on the sofa,','with his leg raised on a pillow.','He thanked Adam for helping,','and said that his father, Sameh, and Abu Sameh had stood by them as though they had known them for years.','Adam told him that was only natural,','because they were neighbors.','He stayed for a while.','During the conversation,','he learned more about the family.','Abu Yousef worked as a carpenter,','but work had been scarce for months.','That was why,','they had left their old home for an apartment with lower rent.','Yousef carried a large share of the household expenses.','He had a younger brother named Hamza,','who was still at school,','and spent most of his time in his room.','That was why,','Adam had hardly seen him.','Near the end of their conversation,','Yousef laughed and said:','“By the way,','I know we disturbed you during our first few days.','Adam was surprised.','Yousef continued,','and said his mother had warned him about the music,','but he had thought the sound would not reach the apartment below.','Adam nodded.','He calmly said that the sound did reach them,','especially at night.','Yousef apologized immediately.','He did not defend himself.','And he did not accuse Adam of exaggerating.','He simply said:','“You’re right, and I’m sorry.','It won’t happen again.','Adam felt relieved.','All the anxiety he had felt about this conversation turned out to be unnecessary.','Before he left,','Yousef thanked him again for helping his father.','Adam walked downstairs,','thinking about how easily someone could form a complete picture of another person without knowing anything about them.','Two days earlier,','in Adam’s mind, Yousef had been:','the noisy neighbor.','Now,','he had a name,','a job,','a family,','and worries like anyone else.'];

jarJdidPageTranslations[6] = `A week passed, and Abu Yousef remained home resting his leg. During that time, Yousef began greeting Adam whenever they met on the stairs—sometimes in the morning as they left for work, and sometimes at night as they returned.

One Thursday, Adam went up to the roof carrying a glass of tea. He found Yousef standing at its edge, looking over the neighborhood. Adam greeted him and sat in the old chair. Yousef looked at it, laughed, and asked whether it belonged to the building. Adam explained that he had brought it from his old home and had been sitting in it for years.

Sameh joined them a little later. The three sat together on the roof. Their conversation began with work, moved to transportation, and then to football. Adam discovered that Yousef supported the same team as Sameh, so the two of them began arguing with Adam about the matches.

It was an ordinary conversation, and nothing important happened. But when Adam returned to his apartment, he realized that he had spent more than an hour with Yousef without noticing the time.

That evening, Nour asked, “Has the noisy neighbor become your friend?” Adam laughed and said that at least he was no longer noisy. Nour agreed that the music really had stopped. Since Adam spoke to Yousef, no loud noise had come from the apartment. Even when Yousef’s friends visited on Friday, they laughed quietly and the home was silent before ten.

Adam had not expected the problem to be solved so easily. He remembered how long he had worried before speaking and how many possibilities he had imagined: Yousef might become upset, answer rudely, or cause a problem between the two families.

In the end, all Adam had needed to say was, “We can hear the noise downstairs.” And the answer had simply been, “You’re right, and I’m sorry.” Sometimes problems do not grow because of what happened. They grow because of the words people leave unsaid.`;
