jarJdidPageTranslations[9] = `Two months later, the door to Yousef’s apartment had become one of the familiar doors in the building for Adam. He no longer passed it wondering who lived behind it. Now he knew.

Sometimes Yousef came down to Adam’s apartment after work, and they sat for half an hour drinking tea. Other times, Adam went upstairs to help Hamza with something on the computer. Abu Yousef’s leg improved and he returned to work. Umm Yousef and Umm Adam began sending food to each other. If Umm Adam made maqluba, a plate went upstairs. If Umm Yousef made ma’amoul, a plate came down. Even Nour, who had been the first to become upset about the neighbors’ noise, began asking about Hamza if she did not see him for a few days.

One day after work, Adam was climbing the stairs when he saw an unfamiliar man at the building entrance holding a paper with an address. The man asked, “Excuse me, where is Abu Yousef’s apartment?” Without thinking, Adam answered, “Third floor, the brown door on the left.”

After the man left, Adam stood still for a moment. He remembered the first time he saw that door. It had been closed, the apartment empty, and he had not known who would live there. Then Yousef arrived with the boxes. The music came, and so did the banging. Adam had spent days wondering how to speak to him. He laughed to himself and continued up to his apartment.

Before Adam opened his door, he heard Yousef calling from above. Yousef stood on the stairs holding a football. “We’re going to play. Are you coming?”

Adam looked at his own door, then at Yousef. He was tired from work and had planned to sit and rest. But after a moment, he smiled. “Give me a minute to change, and I’ll come.”

He entered, put down his bag, and his mother asked where he was going. “With Yousef,” he told her. He did not say, “with the new neighbor,” because Yousef was no longer the new neighbor. He was simply Yousef.

Adam understood something he had not considered when they moved: a new home does not become home merely when you put your belongings inside it. It becomes home when you begin to know the doors around you—who lives behind them, who might knock on yours when they need something, and whose door you can knock on without feeling like a stranger.

Adam changed his clothes and went out. He closed the door behind him and walked downstairs with Yousef. Their laughter filled the building between one step and the next—the same building that had felt entirely unfamiliar to Adam two months earlier.

Now it had neighbors. It had friends. And it had life.`;
