yomAilaPageTranslations[2] = `Adam’s grandmother lived in an old neighborhood, in a house that had been there since before he was born. Its door was made of green iron. Although the paint had peeled in several places, his grandmother refused to replace it. She always said that a door did not need replacing if it was not broken.

When they arrived, they did not need to knock. The door was open, and voices and laughter came from inside. Adam’s uncle Mahmoud was already sitting in the living room with Adam’s father, while his children played in the courtyard.

The moment Adam’s grandmother heard them, she came out of the kitchen wearing an apron over her traditional dress, with flour on her hands. She hugged Nour first, then Adam, and asked why he only came to see her with his mother. Adam tried to defend himself by saying that work took up his time. She was not convinced. “Work comes to an end. Your grandmother cannot keep waiting forever.” Adam laughed and kissed her head, knowing she would say the same thing next time.

Everything inside the house had a familiar smell: coffee, cooking, and the old bar of soap beside the sink. The living-room wall was covered with years of family photographs—weddings, graduations, and young children who were adults now.

Adam appeared in several of them. In one he was five, standing beside a tree in the courtyard. In another he wore his school uniform. In his graduation picture, he stood between his parents while his grandmother held his hand.

A little later, his aunt Amal arrived with her husband and daughters. The home became more crowded, and every new arrival made the voices louder. After half an hour, Adam could no longer tell who was speaking to whom. But that was normal. A family gathering did not need anyone to organize it. Everyone talked, everyone heard half of what was said, and in the end everyone was happy.`;
