yomAilaPageTranslations[3] = `The kitchen was the busiest place in the house. Adam’s grandmother had prepared most of the food early, but Umm Adam and Aunt Amal went in to help her. A large pot sat on the stove, bowls of salad covered the table, and beside the sink a pile of dishes waited for someone to wash them.

Nour tried to enter the kitchen for a taste of the food, but her mother quickly sent her out. Adam was sitting in the courtyard with his cousins. One of them, Karim, was eighteen and now in his first year at university. He kept asking Adam about work: How much was his salary? What was the manager like? Was working better than university?

Adam did not like the salary question, but he knew his relatives had no problem asking about anything. He told Karim that work was neither better nor worse than university; it was simply different. At university, people worried about exams. At work, they worried about the end of the month, the manager, and making mistakes. Karim laughed. “So there’s no peace anywhere.” Adam replied, “That’s the conclusion.”

At that moment, his grandmother came into the courtyard and called the young men to carry in the meal. Everyone pretended not to hear. She raised her voice, and within seconds they were all standing. Adam carried a tray of dishes, Karim carried the pot of rice, and Uncle Mahmoud carried the water jug.

In the living room, they spread the meal on the floor because the dining table could not hold the whole family. Everyone chose a place. The small children argued about who would sit beside their grandmother. The adults claimed they did not care where they sat, although each one searched for their usual favorite place.

Adam sat beside his father and looked over the meal. There was more food than they could possibly finish. He knew that half an hour later his grandmother would start saying, “Eat—you haven’t eaten anything,” even though everyone would be too full to breathe.`;
yomAilaClauseTranslations[3] = ['The kitchen was the busiest place in the home.','Adam’s grandmother had prepared most of the food early,','but Umm Adam and Aunt Amal went in to help her.','A large pot sat on the stove,','bowls of salad were on the table,','and beside the sink was a pile of dishes waiting for someone to wash them.','Nour tried to enter the kitchen to taste the food,','but her mother quickly sent her out.','Adam was sitting in the courtyard with his cousins.','One of them,','Karim,','was eighteen years old,','and was now in his first year at university.','He kept asking Adam about work.','How much was the salary?','What was the manager like?','And was working better than university?','Adam did not like the salary question,','but he knew his relatives had no problem asking about anything.','He told Karim that work was neither better nor worse than university.','It was simply different.','At university,','people worried about exams.','At work,','they worried about the end of the month, the manager, and making mistakes.','Karim laughed,','and said:','“So there’s no peace anywhere.','Adam replied:','“That’s the conclusion.','At that moment,','his grandmother came into the courtyard,','and called the young men to carry in the meal.','Everyone pretended not to hear.','His grandmother raised her voice.','Within seconds,','they were all standing.','Adam carried a tray of dishes.','Karim carried the pot of rice.','And Uncle Mahmoud carried the water jug.','In the living room,','they spread the meal on the floor,','because the dining table could not hold the whole family.','Everyone sat in a place.','The small children were arguing about who would sit beside their grandmother.','And the adults said they did not care where they sat,','but each one was searching for the place they usually liked.','Adam sat beside his father.','He looked over the meal.','There was more food than they could possibly finish.','And he knew that half an hour later his grandmother would start saying:','“Eat,','you haven’t eaten anything.','Even though everyone would be too full to breathe.'];

yomAilaPageTranslations[4] = `The first ten minutes of lunch were relatively quiet because everyone was busy eating. Then the conversation began. Adam’s uncle started talking about prices, and his father answered with something about work. Aunt Amal began talking about her neighbor, while Umm Adam listened and simultaneously watched whether Nour was eating properly.

His grandmother left nobody alone. Whenever Adam stopped eating, she reached over and added more to his plate. He told her several times that he was full. She always replied, “That’s not food.” Adam looked at his full plate and wondered what food looked like to his grandmother if this did not count.

At the edge of the meal, one of Aunt Amal’s young children refused to eat. His mother kept trying: once saying it would help him grow, once saying it would make him strong, and once threatening that there would be no dessert unless he ate. The child continued refusing. Then his grandmother came, placed a bite in his mouth with her own hand, and he ate it without any trouble.

Adam watched and laughed. He did not know her secret, but his grandmother could make children do things they would never do for their parents.

Near the end of lunch, Uncle Mahmoud brought up Adam’s marriage. Adam had a glass of water at his mouth and almost choked. His uncle said that Adam had a job now, so all that remained was to find him a bride. Nour laughed loudly and wiggled her eyebrows when Adam looked at her.

His mother tried to change the subject, but his grandmother joined the conversation. She said she wanted to celebrate his wedding before she died. Adam covered his face with his hand. He knew there was no answer to that sentence. If he said she would live a long life, she would say that lifespans were in God’s hands. If he said he was not ready, it would begin an endless discussion.

So he chose the safest answer. He reached toward the food and said, “Give me a little more.” Everyone around the meal laughed, and his grandmother smiled as though she knew he had escaped the question.`;
