yomAilaPageTranslations[5] = `After they finished eating, everyone wanted to stand, but nobody could move easily. The men sat in the courtyard drinking coffee. The women began clearing the meal and washing the dishes. The children went back to running around as though they had eaten nothing.

Adam tried to escape to the courtyard, but his mother called him from the kitchen. A pile of dishes stood before her. He looked at the dishes, then at her. “Mum, I’m a guest.” She looked back at him. “A guest in your grandmother’s home?” He had no answer.

He stood beside the sink and began drying the dishes his mother washed. Aunt Amal packed the leftover food into containers. His grandmother stood in the kitchen doorway insisting that Umm Adam take half the food home. Umm Adam refused; his grandmother insisted. Adam knew who would win. Half an hour later, three containers were ready to leave with them.

When Adam finished drying the dishes, he went into the courtyard. His father and uncle were talking about the past, remembering when they were young and how the same courtyard had seemed larger through their eyes.

Adam sat beside them and listened. His uncle pointed to a wall at the edge of the courtyard and said they used to climb over it onto the neighbors’ roof. His father said that once their father caught them and chased them around the courtyard. His uncle laughed and said Abu Adam had run away and left him alone.

Adam looked at his father. It was difficult to imagine the man he had always known as calm and serious running from his own father as a child. But the more stories Adam heard, the more differently he began to see him.

Before he became Adam’s father, he had been a boy. He had friends, problems, and days when he came home to his mother afraid of punishment.

Adam thought that perhaps this was the loveliest thing about a family gathering—not the food or the sweets, but the stories that only emerged when people sat together.`;
