yomAilaPageTranslations[6] = `Near the afternoon, Adam’s grandmother took an old photo album from the cupboard. It was large, with a dark-red cover. Its corners were worn and some photographs inside had turned yellow.

Nour sat beside her grandmother on the sofa, and Adam sat on the other side. The first photographs showed his grandparents when they were young. In one, his grandmother wore an embroidered dress and had long black hair. Adam looked at her, then back at the photograph. “Is that you?” His grandmother pretended to be offended. “No, it’s our neighbor.” Nour burst out laughing.

His grandmother continued turning the pages. Photographs appeared of Umm Adam as a child, including one where she wore a full skirt and had her hair tied on both sides. Adam called his mother and showed it to her. She tried to take the album, but Nour pulled it to her chest.

They began searching for the most embarrassing photographs. They found Uncle Mahmoud with long hair, Adam’s father with a strange mustache, and Aunt Amal wearing enormous glasses. Every time they found one, they called its subject over. The living room filled with laughter.

Eventually they reached photographs of Adam and Nour. Adam found one of himself as a baby sleeping on his stomach, and another of him crying at his birthday. Nour immediately photographed it with her phone. Adam tried to grab the phone, but she ran away. She told him the photograph now belonged to the family and she would use it against him at the right time.

After the laughter, his grandmother took back the album. She quietly turned several pages and stopped at a photograph of their grandfather. She looked at it for a while. The house remained loud around her, but she fell silent. Adam noticed. His grandfather had been dead for years.

His grandmother ran a finger over the photograph, then closed the album. “May God have mercy on the old days.” For a few seconds, nobody joked. Then the conversation slowly returned. Adam understood that not every photograph was funny. Some opened a door to people who no longer sat among them.`;
