yomAilaPageTranslations[7] = `Shortly before sunset, a problem arose between two of the family’s boys. They had been playing football in the courtyard even though the adults had repeatedly told them not to play near the window. Naturally, they kept playing.

Then everyone heard glass break. The whole house went silent at once. Adam went into the courtyard and found the ball inside a small room beside the living room, with the window shattered. The two boys stood beside the wall, each looking at the other, and neither willing to say who had kicked the ball.

Adam’s uncle arrived, followed by his aunt. Within a minute, everyone was asking the same question: “Who broke the window?” The younger child pointed at his cousin. His cousin shouted that he was lying and that the younger boy had kicked it. They began arguing.

Adam’s grandmother came from the living room. She looked at the glass, then at the two boys. Everyone expected her to become angry, but she asked whether anyone had been hurt. When they said no, she replied, “That’s enough. Glass can be repaired.”

The adults remained silent. Adam’s uncle said the children needed punishment. His grandmother shook her head and said they would clean the courtyard and would not play football there again. Then she returned to the living room as though the matter were finished.

Adam stood beside his father and looked at the window. His father smiled. “I broke that same window when I was Karim’s age.” Adam looked at him quickly. “The same one?” His father nodded and said their father had been angry with him for an entire week.

Adam laughed and understood why his grandmother had not become too angry. Perhaps because the window had broken before, had been repaired, and life had continued.`;
