yomAilaPageTranslations[8] = `After the sunset prayer, everyone began saying they needed to go home. But as with every family gathering, nearly an hour passed between the first “Come on, we need to go” and anyone actually leaving through the door.

Aunt Amal put on her jacket, then sat back down to finish a conversation with Umm Adam. Uncle Mahmoud stood at the courtyard door and began a new conversation with Adam’s father. The children put on their shoes, then went back to playing. Umm Adam searched for the food containers his grandmother had prepared, while the grandmother searched for more bags to send with everyone.

Adam stood ready beside the door for a long time. Whenever he thought they were about to leave, another conversation began. Eventually they stepped into the street. His grandmother remained in the doorway and said goodbye to each person separately, even though they were all standing together.

When Adam’s turn came, she held his hand. “Don’t wait until next Friday. Come on any day.” Adam promised her. He knew that sometimes he made promises, then became buried in work and forgot. But this time, as he looked at her face, he felt that he wanted to try.

They walked down the street. Nour carried a box of sweets, his mother carried a bag of food, and his father was saying they had eaten too much. Adam walked behind them. His stomach was heavy with food and his head was full of voices from all the talking.

He was tired, but it was the kind of tiredness that did not make him unhappy.`;
