yomAilaPageTranslations[9] = `When they arrived home, everyone went to their own place. Nour entered her room with the sweets. His father sat on the sofa and turned on the television. His mother went into the kitchen to put the food in the refrigerator. Adam entered his room and changed his clothes.

He sat on the edge of his bed and opened his phone. Nour had sent the family group a photograph of Adam crying at his birthday as a child. His uncle had written a laughing comment beneath it, and his aunt sent another photograph. Within seconds, the group filled with messages and laughter.

Adam replied to Nour with an old photograph of her as a little girl with her hair sticking up. From her room, he heard her shout, “Adam!” He laughed.

A little later, he closed his phone and sat quietly. All day, his grandmother’s home had been full of noise: conversation, laughter, running children, dishes, coffee, questions about work, and questions about marriage that he wished nobody would ask.

A person could become tired of all those sounds. But when Adam returned to his quiet room, he felt that the chaos had a flavor that silence did not.

He remembered his grandmother adding more food to his plate, his father talking about his childhood, Nour running away with the phone, the children after they broke the window, and the old photographs.

Every scene had been simple. Nothing major had happened that day. There was no news that changed their lives, no surprise, and no serious problem. But perhaps that was why some days remained in memory: because they were ordinary days, and all the people they loved were there.

Before sleeping, he opened his phone again and opened his conversation with his grandmother. He wrote, “Maybe I’ll stop by after work on Tuesday.” He looked at the message for a moment, then pressed send.

Less than a minute later, the reply came: “Come, sweetheart. But don’t eat dinner—I’ll make you something.” Adam laughed. He knew that even if he said he had eaten, his grandmother would still make him food.

He put the phone aside and closed his eyes. Friday was over, but the conversations, photographs, and laughter remained with him. Perhaps that was what remained most from a day with the family.`;
