const azoumaPageTranslations = [
`A few weeks after the last gathering at Adam’s grandmother’s home, Umm Adam decided that this time it was their turn to host.

She was standing in the kitchen on Tuesday, stirring a pot on the stove, when she calmly asked, “What do you think about inviting the family here for lunch on Friday?”

Adam was sitting at the table drinking tea. The moment he heard the question, he raised his head. Nour, who had been holding her phone, looked up as well. Abu Adam fell silent for a moment, then looked around the living room.

The new apartment was larger than their old home, but the family was large: Uncle Mahmoud, his wife and children; Aunt Amal, her husband and daughters; Adam’s grandmother; and perhaps his other uncle too, if he was not busy.

Adam counted them in his head, then looked at his mother. “All of them?” She looked at him as though the question were strange. “Yes, all of them.”

Nour laughed. “Where are we going to seat them—on the roof?” Her mother said that the living room was large enough and that they could spread the meal on the floor as they always did.

Abu Adam did not object. He said the new home needed to be filled with people, not remain tidy and empty. Umm Adam smiled. From that moment, the invitation was decided.

But Adam knew one thing: for his mother, an invitation did not begin on Friday. It began the day she decided to invite everyone.

The next day, he found a sheet of paper on the refrigerator door with a long list: rice, meat, chicken, yogurt, vegetables, drinks, sweets, coffee, and tea.

Beneath the list, written in larger letters, were the words: “Adam—market, Thursday after work.”

Adam kept staring at his name. Then he called out, “Mum! Why is my name written there?” His mother’s voice came from the kitchen: “So you don’t forget.”

Adam sighed. The invitation was still two days away, but he was already tired.`,
  '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', ''
];
