azoumaPageTranslations[1] = `On Thursday, Adam finished work at four thirty. He had planned to go straight home, but as soon as he opened his phone, he found a message from his mother: “Don’t forget the market.” Beneath it, she had sent a photograph of the list. Adam laughed. It was clear that she did not trust him to remember.

He stopped at the market before going home. It was busier than usual because everyone was buying what they needed for Friday. The vendors called loudly: one advertising tomatoes, another cucumbers, and a man outside the sweet shop trying to convince every passerby that his knafeh was the best in Gaza.

Adam checked the list. He first went to the vegetable shop and bought tomatoes, cucumbers, onions, parsley, and mint. Then he went to the butcher. A large crowd was waiting, so he waited his turn.

When his turn came, he called his mother to confirm the quantity. She gave him a number. Adam looked at the butcher, then asked again, “All of that?” His mother replied, “Yes. Do you want the guests to leave hungry?” Adam knew that was not a real question.

He bought the meat, then chicken, yogurt, and drinks. Every time he checked something off the list, he discovered something else. Half an hour later, he was holding four large bags. He told himself that he was finished.

But before he left the market, his phone rang. It was his mother. The first thing she said was, “I forgot to tell you…” Adam closed his eyes. He knew the trip was not over yet.

She asked him to bring nuts, two packets of coffee, and a small plate of a particular sweet his grandmother liked. Adam tried to remind her that she was already planning to make dessert at home. His mother replied, “That is one thing, and this is another.” Adam did not understand the difference, but he bought what she requested.

When he returned home, he carried bags in both hands and a small bag hung from one finger. He struggled to open the door. Nour saw him and began laughing. “Did you go shopping for a lunch invitation or a wedding?”

Adam placed the bags on the floor. “Ask your mother.”

His mother came from the kitchen and began searching through the bags. A minute later, she asked, “Where are the lemons?” Adam froze and looked at the list. The lemons were written there—and he had forgotten them.`;
azoumaClauseTranslations[1] = ['On Thursday,','Adam finished work at four thirty.','He had planned to go straight home,','but as soon as he opened his phone,','he found a message from his mother:','“Don’t forget the market.','Beneath it,','she had sent a photograph of the list.','Adam laughed.','It was clear that she did not trust him.','He stopped at the market before going home.','The market was busier than usual,','because everyone was buying what they needed for Friday.','The vendors called loudly.','One was advertising tomatoes.','Another was advertising cucumbers.','And a man outside the sweet shop was trying to convince everyone that his knafeh was the best in Gaza.','Adam checked the list.','First, he went to the vegetable shop.','He bought tomatoes,','cucumbers,','onions,','parsley,','and mint.','Then he went to the butcher.','A large crowd was waiting,','so he waited his turn.','When his turn came,','he called his mother to confirm the quantity.','His mother gave him a number.','Adam looked at the butcher,','then asked again:','“All of that?','His mother replied:','“Yes.','Do you want the guests to leave hungry?','Adam knew that was not a real question.','He bought the meat.','Then he bought chicken.','Then yogurt.','Then drinks.','Every time he checked something off the list,','he discovered something else.','Half an hour later,','he was holding four large bags.','He told himself that he was finished.','But before he left the market,','his phone rang.','His mother.','He answered.','The first thing she said was:','“I forgot to tell you…” Adam closed his eyes.','He knew the trip was not over yet.','She asked him to bring nuts,','two packets of coffee,','and a small plate of a particular sweet his grandmother liked.','Adam tried to remind her that she was already planning to make dessert at home.','His mother replied:','“That is one thing,','and this is another.','Adam did not understand the difference.','But he bought what she requested.','When he returned home,','he carried bags in both hands,','with a small bag hanging from one finger.','He struggled to open the door.','Nour saw him,','and began laughing.','“Did you go shopping for a lunch invitation or a wedding?','Adam placed the bags on the floor.','He said:','“Ask your mother.','His mother came from the kitchen,','and began searching through the bags.','A minute later,','she asked:','“Where are the lemons?','Adam froze.','He looked at the list.','The lemons were written there.','And he had forgotten them.'];

azoumaPageTranslations[2] = `After Adam returned to the market for the lemons, he declared that his part was finished. He wanted to change his clothes and sleep early, but his mother had other plans. The living room needed arranging, the large table had to be moved aside, the chairs distributed, and the rooms cleaned—even though Adam did not understand why the guests would see his room.

Nour was assigned the living room, but disappeared after ten minutes. Adam found her in her bedroom holding her phone. She claimed she was taking a short break. When he reminded her that she had only started ten minutes earlier, she said cleaning was tiring.

In the kitchen, his mother washed vegetables, soaked rice, and prepared spices, trying to finish as much as possible that evening. When Abu Adam returned from work and saw the home turned upside down, he joked, “Are we inviting people, or moving house again?” Umm Adam did not laugh; she told him to help instead of making jokes.

Soon everyone was working, though Umm Adam still moved between them giving instructions. By the end of the night, Adam collapsed onto the sofa, more tired than after a full workday. His mother inspected the tidy living room, checked the nearly midnight clock, and told everyone to sleep early because tomorrow would be a long day. Adam could not tell whether she was joking. As far as he was concerned, the long day had already ended.`;
azoumaClauseTranslations[2] = ['After Adam returned to the market and brought the lemons,','he said his part was finished.','He wanted to enter his room,','change his clothes,','and sleep early.','But his mother had other plans.','The living room needed arranging.','The large table had to be moved aside.','The chairs needed to be distributed.','And the rooms needed cleaning,','although Adam did not understand why the guests would see his room in the first place.','Nour was assigned to clean the living room,','but after ten minutes she disappeared.','Adam found her in her room holding her phone.','He stood in the doorway.','“Aren’t you supposed to be cleaning?','Nour replied that she was taking a short break.','Adam looked at the clock.','“You only started ten minutes ago.','She said cleaning was tiring.','Adam looked at her,','then left without replying.','In the kitchen,','his mother was beginning the preparations.','She washed the vegetables.','She soaked the rice.','And prepared the spices.','She was trying to finish as much as possible that evening so the work would not pile up in the morning.','Abu Adam returned from work,','and when he entered, he saw the home turned upside down.','He stood in the living-room doorway.','He said:','“Are we inviting people, or moving house again?','Umm Adam did not laugh.','She told him that instead of making jokes,','he should help.','Half an hour later,','Abu Adam was arranging the chairs.','Adam was wiping the table.','Nour had finally left her room.','And his mother was in the kitchen.','Although everyone was doing a task,','Umm Adam still moved between them all.','“Adam,','don’t put the chair there.','“Nour,','the mirror is still dusty.','“Abu Adam,','this table needs to go on the other side.','At the end of the night,','Adam sat on the sofa.','He was more tired than after a full workday.','His mother came out of the kitchen,','and inspected the living room.','Thank God,','everything was tidy.','Then she looked at the clock.','It was nearly midnight.','She said:','“Come on, go to sleep early.','Tomorrow we have a long day.','Adam looked at her.','He did not know whether she meant it as a joke.','Because as far as he was concerned,','the long day had already ended.'];

azoumaPageTranslations[3] = `At seven Friday morning, Umm Adam was awake. By seven thirty, the pots were on the stove. At eight, Adam woke to the smell of onions and spices. He stayed in bed listening to cupboards opening and closing, the blender, and a pot being placed on the stove. It was clear the whole home was awake.

In the kitchen, his mother stood among several pots, her hair tied and flour on her apron. Adam asked whether she needed help and immediately regretted it when she said yes. She handed him parsley to chop. First it was not fine enough; then she warned him not to chop it into mush. When he told her to do it herself, she said everyone had to learn.

After the parsley came cucumbers, lemons, and a trip downstairs for bread. When Adam returned, Nour was awake and fixing her hair in the living-room mirror. He complained that they had been working since eight while she fixed her hair. She replied that her mother had asked her to arrange the sitting room and she had finished. Adam saw that it really was tidy and had no answer.

Around ten, Aunt Amal called to say they might come early to help. Umm Adam was delighted. Looking at all the pots, Adam thought the help should have arrived yesterday.`;
azoumaClauseTranslations[3] = ['At seven in the morning,','Umm Adam was awake.','At seven thirty,','the pots were on the stove.','And at eight,','Adam woke to the smell of onions and spices.','He stayed in bed for a few minutes.','He could hear his mother opening and closing the kitchen cupboards.','Then the sound of the blender.','Then the sound of a pot being placed on the stove.','He tried to go back to sleep,','but it was clear that the whole home was awake.','He got up.','When he entered the kitchen,','he found his mother standing among several pots.','Her hair was tied,','and there was flour on her apron.','He asked whether she needed help.','She said:','“Yes.','Adam quickly regretted asking.','She gave him a bunch of parsley,','and asked him to chop it.','He sat at the table,','and began.','A minute later,','his mother looked at the parsley.','She said:','“Finer than that.','Adam chopped it again.','She looked a second time.','“Not like that,','you’ll turn it to mush.','He put down the knife.','“Mum,','do it yourself.','His mother became annoyed.','She said a person had to learn.','Adam picked up the knife again.','After the parsley,','he peeled cucumbers.','Then he cut lemons.','Then he went downstairs to buy bread.','When he returned,','Nour was awake.','She stood before the living-room mirror fixing her hair.','Adam looked at her.','“We’ve been working since eight,','and you’re fixing your hair?','Nour said her mother had asked her to arrange the sitting room,','and that she had finished.','Adam looked at the living room.','It really was tidy.','He found nothing to say.','Around ten o’clock,','his mother’s phone rang.','It was Aunt Amal.','She said they might come a little earlier to help.','His mother was delighted.','After ending the call,','she said:','“Thank God,','Amal is coming to help me.','Adam looked at the pots.','In his view,','the help should have arrived yesterday.'];

azoumaPageTranslations[4] = `At around eleven thirty, Aunt Amal arrived with her two daughters. Although she said she had come to help, she carried a large tray of sweets. Umm Adam immediately asked why she had gone to the trouble. Amal replied, “What trouble? It’s only a little dessert.” Adam thought this was exactly what his mother said whenever she visited someone carrying half the market.

Aunt Amal entered the kitchen, her daughters sat with Nour, and Adam tried to rest in his room before everyone else arrived. Within fifteen minutes, the bell rang again. Uncle Mahmoud arrived with his wife and children, and Adam’s grandmother followed ten minutes later.

Umm Adam hugged her mother. Adam took the bag she carried and discovered food inside. “Grandma, you’re invited to lunch and you brought food?” She said she had made something small and decided to bring it. Umm Adam kept insisting she should not have troubled herself.

Adam laughed to himself. Every guest had brought something: sweets, food, drinks, or salad. In the end, perhaps his mother had not needed to cook at all.

With the guests’ arrival, the home changed. The living room filled, voices rose, children moved from room to room, and everyone wanted to see the new apartment. Aunt Amal praised the balcony. Adam’s grandmother went directly into the kitchen and opened the pots. She tasted the broth despite Umm Adam’s protest, then announced that it needed a little salt. Umm Adam fell silent while Adam tried not to laugh.`;
azoumaClauseTranslations[4] = ['At around eleven thirty,','the doorbell rang.','It was Aunt Amal with her two daughters.','Although she said she had come to help,','she was carrying a large tray of sweets.','The moment Umm Adam saw the sweets,','she said:','“Why did you trouble yourself?','Her aunt replied:','“What trouble?','It’s only a little dessert.','Adam was standing nearby,','and thought that this was exactly what his mother said when she visited someone carrying half the market.','Aunt Amal entered the kitchen,','and women’s voices began coming from inside.','His cousin sat with Nour.','Adam entered his room,','and tried to sit for a while before everyone else arrived.','But fifteen minutes had not passed,','when the bell rang again.','This time,','it was Uncle Mahmoud with his wife and children.','Ten minutes after them,','his grandmother arrived.','The moment she entered,','Umm Adam went to her and hugged her.','Adam took the bag she was carrying.','He looked inside.','There was a container of food.','He asked:','“Grandma,','are you coming to a lunch invitation and bringing food?','His grandmother said she had made something small and decided to bring it.','Umm Adam took the container,','and kept saying she should not have troubled herself.','Adam laughed to himself.','Everyone coming to the invitation was carrying something.','His aunt brought sweets.','His grandmother brought food.','His uncle brought drinks.','And his uncle’s wife brought a bowl of salad.','In the end,','perhaps his mother did not need to cook anything at all.','With the guests’ arrival,','the home changed.','The living room filled.','The voices rose.','The children began moving from room to room.','And everyone wanted to see the new home.','His aunt walked around the apartment,','and praised the balcony.','His grandmother entered the kitchen,','and the first thing she did was open the pots.','Umm Adam looked at her.','“Mum,','leave the food alone.','His grandmother did not reply.','She tasted some of the broth.','Then she said:','“It needs a little salt.','Umm Adam fell silent.','Adam was standing in the kitchen doorway.','He tried not to laugh.'];

azoumaPageTranslations[5] = `At around one thirty, they began laying out the meal. The dining table was too small, so they spread a long cloth on the living-room floor. Adam and his cousin Karim carried dishes from the kitchen. The first pot was heavy, and Umm Adam followed them warning them to be careful, go slowly, and not tilt it. Adam felt as if they were transporting something priceless.

They brought the dishes, salads, yogurt, and bread. The meal filled the floor, and Adam finally understood where all the market bags had gone.

Everyone began finding a place. The children each wanted to sit beside someone specific. Nour wanted her cousin, one boy wanted Adam, and his grandmother wanted Adam beside her. In the end, he sat between his grandmother and a six-year-old boy—the worst possible position. His grandmother would keep adding food from the right, while the child might spill water on him from the left.

The first few minutes of eating were quiet, but soon the conversations began: work, prices, studies, and whether everyone had taken meat. Adam tried to eat, but someone questioned him every few minutes about his job, manager, time at the company, and future plans. Lunch had become another job interview.

Then his aunt asked about marriage. Adam stopped eating and looked at Nour, who had been waiting for the question and began laughing before anyone answered. “Can’t we have one invitation without that question?” he asked. His grandmother answered, “No.” Everyone laughed. Adam resumed eating, knowing the subject was finished for today—but not for the next invitation.`;
azoumaClauseTranslations[5] = ['At around one thirty,','they began laying out the meal.','The dining table was too small,','so they spread a long cloth on the living-room floor.','Adam and Karim,','his cousin,','began carrying dishes from the kitchen.','The first pot was heavy.','Adam held one side,','and Karim held the other.','Umm Adam kept walking behind them,','warning them:','“Be careful.','“Slowly.','“Don’t tilt it.','Adam felt as though he was not carrying a pot.','He felt as though he was transporting something very valuable.','Then they brought the dishes,','salads,','yogurt,','and bread.','The meal became so full that it reminded Adam of the market.','Now he understood where all the bags had gone.','Everyone began sitting down.','The first problem was with the children.','Each one wanted to sit beside someone specific.','Nour wanted to sit beside her cousin.','One of the children wanted to sit beside Adam.','And his grandmother wanted Adam beside her.','In the end,','Adam sat between his grandmother and a six-year-old child.','He felt the situation could not be worse.','On the right,','his grandmother would keep adding food to his plate.','And on the left,','the child might spill water on him at any moment.','They began eating.','For the first few minutes,','the living room became a little quieter.','But it did not last.','The conversation began.','His uncle talked about work.','His father talked about the market and prices.','His aunt asked Nour about her studies.','And his grandmother made sure everyone had taken meat.','Adam was trying to eat,','but every few minutes someone asked him a question.','How is work?','How is the new manager?','How long have you been at the company?','Do you plan to stay there?','It was as though lunch had become another job interview.','Then his aunt asked him about marriage.','Adam stopped eating.','He looked at Nour.','Nour had been waiting for the question.','She began laughing before anyone said anything.','Adam said:','“Can’t we have one invitation without that question?','His grandmother replied:','“No.','Everyone laughed.','Adam resumed eating.','He knew the subject was over for today.','But not for the next invitation.'];

azoumaPageTranslations[6] = `After lunch, the home was even more chaotic: dishes, glasses, playing children, and adults asking for coffee. Umm Adam insisted on tidying quickly while her relatives helped in the kitchen. Adam and Karim collected glasses as two children kept running through the living room despite repeated warnings.

One child collided with Adam’s tray. Water spilled and a glass broke. Everyone fell silent. The frightened child nearly cried, but Adam first moved him away from the glass. Umm Adam came out, took a breath, and—rather than becoming angry—asked whether he was hurt. When he said no, she sent him to sit down and they cleaned up.

Adam remembered his grandmother’s reaction when her window broke: first ask whether anyone was hurt; everything else could be repaired. Ten minutes later, the child was playing again. The glass belonged to a new set, yet Umm Adam simply said, “As long as he’s safe. A glass can be replaced.” Adam realized that once the home filled with people, its belongings were no longer the most important thing.`;
azoumaClauseTranslations[6] = ['After lunch,','the home became even more chaotic than before.','The dishes were on the table.','Glasses of water were everywhere.','The children were playing.','And the adults wanted coffee.','Umm Adam insisted on tidying everything quickly.','His aunt and his uncle’s wife entered the kitchen to help her.','His grandmother sat on the sofa,','and Umm Adam refused to let her stand.','Adam and Karim began collecting the water glasses.','As they worked,','two children were running from one end of the living room to the other.','Umm Adam told them more than once:','“Don’t run inside.','Naturally,','they kept running.','Adam was holding a tray with four glasses.','As he walked toward the kitchen,','one child turned beside the sofa and collided with him.','The tray tilted.','One glass fell.','Then the second.','Water spilled on the floor.','And one glass broke.','Everyone fell silent.','The child froze.','His face changed,','and he was about to cry.','Adam placed the tray on the table,','and first moved the child away from the glass.','His mother came from the kitchen.','When she saw the water and glass,','she took a breath.','Adam expected her to become angry.','But she looked at the child and asked whether he was hurt.','When he said no,','she told him to go sit down.','Then they began cleaning.','Adam remembered his grandmother on the day her window broke.','The same thing.','The first question:','“Was anyone hurt?','After that, everything could be repaired.','Once they dried the floor,','the conversation returned.','The child forgot the matter after ten minutes,','and went back to playing.','Adam looked at the broken glass in the rubbish bag.','It belonged to a new set his mother had bought for the home.','He expected her to be more upset.','But later he heard her tell his aunt:','“As long as he’s safe.','It’s only a glass and can be replaced.','Adam smiled.','Since moving into the new home,','his mother had worried about everything in it.','The new sofa.','The mirror.','The glasses.','But once the home filled with people,','it became clear that its belongings were not the most important thing.'];

azoumaPageTranslations[7] = `After the afternoon prayer, the home became quieter. The tired children settled with the television or their phones. Coffee was served, and Abu Adam, Uncle Mahmoud, and Adam sat on the balcony in the pleasant air.

His uncle praised the apartment and quiet neighborhood. Abu Adam said they had struggled to adjust at first, especially Adam, who used to sit silently on the roof as if they had moved to another country. Adam protested but could not deny that he had missed the old home.

Now, sitting on the balcony with family voices inside, the new home no longer felt strange. His grandmother joined them, accepted Adam’s chair, looked at the street, and said, “A lovely home.” When Umm Adam asked whether she liked it, she replied, “A home becomes beautiful through the people in it.”

Adam looked at his exhausted but happy mother and understood why she worked so hard for one invitation. It was not about food, tidiness, or praise. She wanted the home filled with the voices of people she loved.`;
azoumaClauseTranslations[7] = ['After the afternoon prayer,','the home became a little quieter.','The children grew calmer from tiredness.','Some sat watching television.','And some held their phones.','Umm Adam and his aunt brought out the coffee.','Abu Adam and Uncle Mahmoud went onto the balcony.','Adam sat with them.','The air was pleasant,','and sounds from the neighborhood rose from below.','His uncle looked at the street,','and said the new home was good,','and the neighborhood was quiet.','Abu Adam nodded.','He said that at first they had not adjusted quickly,','especially Adam.','Adam objected.','“Why me?','His father laughed.','He said that during the first week Adam would go to the roof and remain silent as though they had moved to another country.','His uncle laughed.','Adam could not deny it.','He truly had been attached to the old home.','But now,','as he sat on the balcony hearing his family inside,','he did not feel the new home was strange.','A little later,','his grandmother came onto the balcony.','Adam stood to give her his chair.','She sat,','and looked at the street.','She said:','“A lovely home.','Umm Adam heard her from inside,','and came out.','She asked:','“Do you like it?','His grandmother replied:','“A home becomes beautiful through the people in it.','They were silent for a moment.','Adam looked at his mother.','She clearly looked tired.','She had been preparing for two days.','And had been on her feet since morning.','But now,','seeing her mother, siblings, and their children sitting in her home,','she was happy.','Adam understood why his mother worked so hard for one invitation.','It was not about the food.','Nor arranging the home.','Nor having the guests praise the cooking.','She wanted the home filled with the voices of people she loved.'];

azoumaPageTranslations[8] = `Near sunset, Aunt Amal first announced, “Come on, we need to go.” Adam checked the time, knowing they would not leave yet. Jackets went on, then conversations resumed. People searched for phones, chargers, and shoes. His grandmother refused to leave until Umm Adam packed food for her, insisting that the food she had brought that morning was “different.” Adam finally understood where his mother got that phrase.

Half an hour later, they genuinely began leaving. At the apartment door, everyone exchanged individual goodbyes. Umm Adam thanked them for coming while they praised her cooking and insisted she had worked too hard—something she denied.

Adam thought of the market, forgotten lemons, preparations, early morning, pots, dishes, and broken glass. In his view, his mother had worked very hard, but she did not seem to see it that way.

At the door, his grandmother held Adam’s face, kissed him, and said, “May God grant you happiness in your home.” After they left, Adam listened to their steps disappear down the stairs, closed the door, turned toward the living room, and was struck by the sudden silence.`;
azoumaClauseTranslations[8] = ['Near sunset,','Aunt Amal said the first words:','“Come on,','we need to go.','Adam looked at the clock.','He knew they would not leave yet.','His aunt put on her jacket,','but then sat again to finish a conversation.','His uncle’s wife began searching for her phone.','Karim was searching for his charger.','And one child could not find his shoe.','His grandmother refused to leave before Umm Adam packed some food for her.','Umm Adam objected.','“Mum,','you brought food with you this morning!','His grandmother replied:','“That is different from this.','Adam heard the phrase,','and smiled.','Now he understood where his mother got it.','Half an hour later,','they truly began leaving.','They stood at the apartment door.','Everyone greeted everyone,','although they had spent the entire day together.','Umm Adam kept saying:','“You brightened our home.','And his aunt replied:','“Bless your hands.','And his uncle’s wife said:','“You worked far too hard.','And Umm Adam replied:','“What work?','Adam stood beside the door,','listening.','He thought about Thursday.','The market.','The bags.','The lemons he had forgotten.','Arranging the living room.','The early morning.','The pots.','The dishes.','The glass that broke.','In his view,','his mother had worked very hard.','But clearly she did not see it that way.','When his grandmother reached the door,','she held Adam’s face between her hands,','and kissed him.','She said:','“May God grant you happiness in your home.','Then they left.','Adam remained at the apartment door listening to their footsteps on the stairs until they disappeared.','Then he closed the door.','And turned toward the living room.','The silence came all at once.'];

azoumaPageTranslations[9] = `The living room that had been full half an hour earlier was now empty, though traces of the invitation remained everywhere: a forgotten glass, a tissue, a toy beneath the sofa, and two pieces of dessert.

Adam and Nour collapsed onto the sofas. When Nour declared that nobody should ask her to tidy now, her mother answered from the kitchen, “Five minutes, then get up.” Adam laughed.

He soon joined his mother at the dishes without being asked. She smiled silently. Nour joined them, and Abu Adam arranged chairs. Little by little, the home returned to normal—but not completely, because it now held new memories: where his grandmother sat, where the water spilled, coffee on the balcony, and the first large meal spread across the floor.

Afterward they drank tea together in exhausted silence. Adam suggested ordering ready-made food next time. His mother reacted as though he had proposed something forbidden: “Ready-made food? For the family?” The proposal died immediately.

Yet Adam remembered his grandmother on the balcony, his uncle’s laughter, the children, and the full living room. They might work for two days for a few hours, but perhaps those few hours were what remained.

His mother then announced that next Friday’s invitation was at Aunt Amal’s. Adam sighed—but this time he had no shopping, cooking, or arranging. His only task was to arrive hungry.`;
azoumaClauseTranslations[9] = ['The living room that had been full of people half an hour earlier,','was now empty.','But traces of the invitation were everywhere.','A glass forgotten on the windowsill.','A tissue on the floor.','A small toy beneath the sofa.','And a dessert plate with two pieces left.','Adam sat on the sofa.','He was tired,','and felt he could sleep where he was.','Nour sat on the other sofa.','She looked at the mess.','She said:','“Nobody tell me to tidy now.','From the kitchen,','her mother’s voice came:','“Five minutes, then get up.','Nour closed her eyes.','Adam laughed.','A little later,','he stood and entered the kitchen.','His mother stood before a pile of dishes.','Although his aunt and his uncle’s wife had washed many of them,','work remained.','Adam took a towel.','He began drying dishes without his mother asking.','She looked at him.','She smiled.','She said nothing.','Ten minutes later,','Nour joined them too.','The three of them were in the kitchen.','His father arranged the chairs in the living room.','Little by little,','the home returned to normal.','But not completely.','Because from that day,','the place held new memories.','Here his grandmother had sat.','Here the water had spilled.','They drank coffee on the balcony.','And on the floor they spread the first large meal in the new home.','When they finished,','his mother made tea.','They all sat in the living room.','Nobody had the energy to talk much.','Adam took his tea,','and rested his head against the sofa.','A little later,','he said:','“Next time, let’s order ready-made food.','His mother looked at him as though he had said something forbidden.','“Ready-made food?','For the family?','Adam knew from her tone that the suggestion had died before he could finish it.','Nour laughed.','His father looked at Adam,','and shook his head as though telling him:','“Don’t try.','Adam drank his tea.','He thought about all the day’s work.','And all the chaos.','And every time his mother had called his name to do something.','But he also thought of his grandmother’s face on the balcony.','And his uncle’s laughter.','And the children.','And the living room full of people.','He smiled.','They might work for two days for a few hours.','But perhaps those few hours were what would remain.','Then his mother said,','while drinking tea:','“Next Friday is at my sister Amal’s home.','Adam raised his head.','“Another invitation?','His mother smiled.','“Yes.','Adam sighed.','But this time,','he had no shopping.','No cooking.','No arranging.','He had only one task:','to arrive hungry.'];

const akhirBasPageTranslations = [
`From early that morning, it was clear that Adam’s day would not go smoothly.

He woke late to his alarm. Instead of leaving home at seven thirty as usual, he did not leave until seven forty-five. His mother stood at the kitchen door telling him to take a sandwich, but Adam was in a hurry. He took two bites, drank half a glass of tea, and left.

As he went downstairs, he tried to put on his jacket and answer a message from Muhammad at the same time. The message was brief: “Be prepared—we have a large order today.” Adam read it and sighed.

He had already planned to leave work on time because his friend Samer had asked him to visit after the shift. They had not sat together for more than two weeks, and Samer kept telling him, “Ever since you started working, you’ve disappeared.” Adam promised every time, only for something else to come up. This time, he was determined to go.

He reached work three minutes before the shift began. Muhammad stood beside his desk and said, “You made it just in time.” Adam put down his bag and turned on the computer. Within ten minutes, the work began.

The large order came from a shop with several branches, and every number and quantity needed careful review. Adam entered the data, Muhammad checked it, Rami prepared the invoices, and Suha communicated with the customer.

Time passed quickly: one, two, three. Adam did not notice the time until his phone rang. It was Samer. Adam answered while still printing a document. Samer asked whether their plan was still on.

Adam checked the clock. It was ten past three. He said he would finish at four thirty and visit afterward. Samer warned him, “Don’t be late. The last bus from here leaves early.” Adam laughed. “Don’t worry. I’ll be on my way home by seven.”

He ended the call, unaware that this was the first promise that day he would be unable to keep.`,
  '', '', '', '', '', '', '', '', ''
];
const akhirBasClauseTranslations = [[
  'From early that morning,','it was clear that Adam’s day would not go smoothly.','He woke late to his alarm,','and instead of leaving home at seven thirty as usual,','he did not leave until seven forty-five.','His mother stood at the kitchen door,','telling him to take a sandwich with him,','but Adam was in a hurry.','He took two bites,','drank half a glass of tea,','and left the home.','As he went downstairs,','he tried to put on his jacket and answer a message from Muhammad at the same time.','The message was brief:','“Be prepared,','we have a large order today.','Adam read it,','and sighed.','He had already planned to leave work on time,','because his friend Samer had asked him to visit after the shift.','They had not sat together for more than two weeks,','and Samer kept telling him:','“Ever since you started working,','you’ve disappeared.','Every time, Adam promised him,','only for something else to come up.','That was why,','this time he was determined to go.','He reached work three minutes before the shift began.','Muhammad stood beside his desk,','and the moment he saw him, he said:','“You made it just in time.','Adam put down his bag,','and turned on the computer.','But ten minutes had not passed,','when the work began.','The large order came from a shop with several branches,','and every number and quantity needed careful review.','Adam began entering the data.','Muhammad checked.','Rami prepared the invoices.','And Suha communicated with the customer.','Time passed quickly.','One.','Two.','Three.','Adam did not notice the time until his phone rang.','Samer.','He answered while still printing a document.','Samer asked whether their plan was still on.','Adam checked the clock.','It was ten past three.','He told him he would finish at four thirty,','and visit afterward.','Samer told him:','“Don’t be late.','The last bus from here leaves early.','Adam laughed.','“Don’t worry.','I’ll be on my way home by seven.','He ended the call.','And without knowing it,','this was the first promise that day he would be unable to keep.'
], null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null, null];

akhirBasPageTranslations[1] = `At four o’clock, Adam was convinced that everything was nearly finished. He only had a few orders left to review; then he could shut down the computer and leave. He checked his phone and messaged Samer: “I’ll be leaving shortly.”

Two minutes later, Mr. Majed came to the desk holding a blue folder. He told Adam there was a problem with one of the invoices and that it had to be resolved before they left. Adam checked the time: five past four. He told himself it might take ten minutes, but the moment he opened the file, he knew it would take longer.

The quantity on the invoice did not match the quantity recorded in the system. Rami said his invoice was correct, and Muhammad said the original order was correct too, so the problem had to be elsewhere. They started checking the order again from the beginning. Adam read one number after another, but his eyes kept drifting to the clock: four twenty, four thirty, four thirty-five. The shift was over, yet nobody got up.

Adam messaged Samer: “I’ve been held up a little—not long.” Samer answered, “Come. I’m waiting for you.” At around five, they found the mistake: one item had been entered twice. They corrected the invoice, Rami checked it, and then Mr. Majed checked it too.

Adam shut down the computer at five fifteen. He was tired, but when he left the building, he felt the day could still work out. Samer lived about half an hour from Adam’s workplace. Adam considered canceling, but he had already promised Samer and apologized several times. He told himself, “I’ll visit for an hour, then go home.”

He took a shared taxi and messaged his mother from the window seat: “I’m going to stop by Samer’s. Don’t wait for me for dinner.” She replied, “Don’t be late.” Adam smiled as he read it. Everyone that day was telling him the same thing—and he was still certain he would not be late.`;
akhirBasClauseTranslations[1] = ['At four o’clock,','Adam was convinced that everything was nearly finished.','He had only a few orders left to review,','then he could shut down the computer and leave.','He checked his phone,','and messaged Samer:','“I’ll be leaving shortly.','Two minutes later,','Mr. Majed came to the desk.','He was holding a blue folder.','He stood beside Adam,','and told him there was a problem with one of the invoices,','and that it had to be resolved before they left.','Adam checked the time.','Five past four.','He told himself it might take ten minutes.','He opened the file.','At first glance,','he knew it would take longer than ten minutes.','The quantity on the invoice did not match the quantity recorded in the system.','Rami said the invoice he had prepared was correct.','Muhammad said the original order was correct too.','So the problem was somewhere else.','They began checking the order again from the beginning.','Adam read one number after another,','but his eyes kept drifting to the clock.','Four twenty.','Four thirty.','Four thirty-five.','The shift was over.','But nobody got up.','Adam messaged Samer:','“I’ve been held up a little.','Not long.','Samer replied:','“Come.','I’m waiting for you.','At around five,','they found the mistake.','One of the items had been entered twice.','They corrected the invoice,','Rami checked it,','and then Mr. Majed checked it.','Adam shut down the computer at five fifteen.','He was tired,','but when he left the building,','he felt the day could still work out.','Samer lived about half an hour from Adam’s workplace.','He considered canceling the visit.','But he had already promised Samer and apologized several times.','He told himself:','“I’ll visit for an hour,','then go home.','He took the shared taxi.','While sitting by the window,','he messaged his mother:','“I’m going to stop by Samer’s.','Don’t wait for me for dinner.','His mother replied:','“Don’t be late.','Adam read the message and smiled.','Everyone that day was telling him the same thing.','And he was convinced he would not be late.'];

akhirBasPageTranslations[2] = `Adam reached Samer’s home at around a quarter to six. Samer was standing outside, and the moment he saw Adam, he opened his arms and exclaimed, “Finally!” Adam laughed. “Come on, it’s been two weeks, not two years.” Samer joked that ever since Adam started working, people had to book an appointment with him a week in advance.

They went inside. Samer’s mother was in the kitchen. When she saw Adam, she warmly greeted him and kept asking about his family. Then she brought out a plate of dessert and two glasses of tea. Adam said he was not hungry, but she did not ask again. She set the plate before him and simply said, “Eat.” Adam knew the discussion was over.

He and Samer sat in the room. At first they talked about work. Samer wanted to know how things were going with Adam’s new manager, and Adam told him about the changes at the office. Then the conversation shifted to their old friends: who had found work, who had traveled, who had become engaged, and who still talked every day about starting a business that nobody had ever seen.

They laughed, then began remembering moments from university. One subject led to another. Every time Adam checked the clock, he would say, “I’ll go home in a little while.” At seven, he said he should leave, but Samer asked him to wait half an hour because he had ordered dinner.

Adam objected: “I told my mother not to wait for me—I didn’t tell her I was sleeping over at your place.” Samer laughed and assured him that the last shared taxi toward his area ran until around eight thirty, so there was still time. Adam thought it over. Half an hour would not be a problem, so he stayed.

Dinner was late and arrived at seven forty-five. They ate, then Samer started another conversation. Adam forgot the time. When he finally remembered and opened his phone, it was eight twenty-seven. Adam jumped to his feet. “Oh, man!” Samer checked the time, and his expression changed. “Come on—quickly.”`;
